## CMS & Website Customization Requirements

Upgrade the admin panel into a **fully dynamic CMS**, allowing the administrator to manage the website content, branding, navigation, homepage sections, links, and custom styling without editing code.

### 1. Logo Management

* Upload/change website logo from the admin panel.
* Support PNG, JPG, SVG, and WebP where appropriate.
* Preview the current logo.
* Replace or remove the logo.
* Logo should automatically update everywhere it is used across the website, including header, footer, login pages, certificates/cards, and other configured locations.

### 2. Website Content Management

Allow administrators to edit **all editable website content dynamically**.

Admin should be able to change:

* Headings
* Subheadings
* Paragraphs
* Button text
* Labels
* Section descriptions
* Contact information
* Footer content
* Copyright text
* Images
* Icons
* Testimonials
* Donor-related content
* Call-to-action content
* Any other CMS-enabled text/content

Changes made in the CMS should immediately reflect on the frontend without requiring code changes.

### 3. Homepage Section-Wise CMS

Make the homepage completely **section-based and dynamically editable**.

Example sections:

* Hero
* About
* Donation Statistics
* Why Donate
* Donor Stories
* Donation Process
* Testimonials
* Campaigns
* Call to Action
* Contact
* Footer

Each section should have its own CMS management interface.

Admin should be able to:

* Edit section content
* Edit section title/subtitle
* Change images
* Change buttons
* Change button links
* Enable/disable sections
* Duplicate sections where appropriate
* Delete sections
* Add new sections
* Rearrange section order using drag-and-drop

### 4. Add Custom Sections

Provide an option:

**+ Add New Section**

The administrator should be able to create a custom section by selecting/configuring:

* Section title
* Description
* Image
* Icon
* Buttons
* Links
* Layout
* Background
* Custom content

The system should support reusable section components rather than hard-coding every homepage block.

### 5. Menu Management

Create a complete **Menu Manager**.

Admin should be able to:

* Add menu item
* Edit menu item
* Delete menu item
* Rename menu item
* Change menu URL
* Add external links
* Link to internal pages
* Link to CMS sections
* Create dropdown menus
* Create nested submenu items
* Rearrange menu items using drag-and-drop
* Enable/disable menu items

Example:

**Home**
**About**
**Donate**
**Donor Stories**
**Campaigns**
**Contact**

The administrator should be able to change this structure without modifying code.

### 6. Link Management

Every CMS button/link should be editable.

For each button or link provide:

* Button text
* URL
* Internal page selection
* External URL
* Open in same tab/new tab
* Enable/disable

Example:

`Donate Now → /donate`

The administrator should be able to change it to:

`Donate Now → /make-a-donation`

without developer intervention.

### 7. Page Management

Create a CMS page manager allowing administrators to:

* Create pages
* Edit pages
* Delete pages
* Publish/unpublish pages
* Change page title
* Change page content
* Upload featured image
* Configure page URL/slug
* Add sections to the page
* Rearrange sections

### 8. Dynamic Homepage Builder

The homepage should not depend on hard-coded static content.

Use a structure similar to:

**Homepage**
→ Section 1
→ Section 2
→ Section 3
→ Section 4
→ Section 5

Each section loads its content from the CMS/database.

Frontend rendering should dynamically loop through the active sections according to their saved order.

### 9. Custom CSS

Add an administrator-controlled:

**Custom CSS**

panel.

Allow the administrator to enter custom CSS that applies to the website.

Provide:

* CSS editor
* Save/update
* Enable/disable custom CSS
* Preview where possible
* Reset/remove custom CSS

The custom CSS should load safely and should not require editing theme/source files.

Optionally provide separate controls for:

* Global CSS
* Homepage CSS
* Individual page CSS

### 10. Custom JavaScript — Optional

If supported, provide a separate administrator-only **Custom JavaScript** area with appropriate security restrictions.

Do not allow ordinary users or donors to inject scripts.

### 11. Media Library

Create a centralized media library for CMS assets.

Admin can:

* Upload images
* Replace images
* Delete images
* Search images
* Reuse existing images
* View image details

CMS sections should be able to select images from this library instead of repeatedly uploading files.

### 12. Visibility Controls

Every homepage section should have:

**Published / Hidden**

status.

Hidden sections should remain saved in the CMS but should not appear on the public website.

### 13. Draft & Publishing

Where practical, support:

**Draft → Preview → Publish**

so administrators can prepare changes before making them publicly visible.

### 14. Database-Driven Architecture

Do not hard-code editable content in templates.

Use a structure such as:

**Pages**

* id
* title
* slug
* status

**Page Sections**

* id
* page_id
* section_type
* section_order
* status
* configuration/content

**Menus**

* id
* name
* location

**Menu Items**

* id
* menu_id
* parent_id
* title
* url
* order
* status

**Media**

* id
* file
* type
* metadata

**Site Settings**

* logo
* favicon
* contact information
* social links
* footer content
* custom CSS

### 15. Important Requirement

The CMS must be **truly dynamic**.

An administrator should be able to change the website's:

**Logo → Content → Images → Buttons → Links → Menus → Homepage Sections → Section Order → Visibility → Pages → Custom CSS**

without requiring a developer to modify the source code.

The frontend should automatically retrieve the latest published CMS data and render the website accordingly.

### Final Admin Experience

**Admin Dashboard**

→ Website Settings
→ Logo & Branding
→ Media Library
→ Pages
→ Homepage Builder
→ Sections
→ Menu Manager
→ Donor Stories
→ Content Manager
→ Custom CSS
→ Site Settings

The goal is to make the website **fully CMS-driven, modular, editable, and scalable**, while keeping the existing frontend design and functionality intact unless explicitly changed by the administrator.
